<%#
  T-32 / FR-61 — the audit. §7b.5's justification in its own words: *"today nothing records
  what left the building. Now there is a log you can answer questions from."*

  Redmine's own chrome only: `h2`, `table.list`, `p.nodata`, `em.info`.

  --- WHAT AN ADMIN SEES AND WHAT EVERYBODY ELSE SEES ---

  The controller narrows a non-admin to their own rows. FR-61 requires the log to be
  visible to ADMINS; showing one holder of `mail_…_reports` who else mailed which report to
  whom would be a new disclosure that no requirement asks for. The sentence below says
  which list is being shown, because a page that silently shows you a subset is a page you
  will later swear was empty.
%>
<h2><%= l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail_audit) %></h2>

<p class="nodata">
  <%= User.current.admin? ? l(:text_reporter_adhoc_audit_scope_all) :
                            l(:text_reporter_adhoc_audit_scope_own) %>
</p>

<% if @mail_sends.any? %>
  <%# BOUNDED, AND THE PAGE SAYS SO WHEN IT IS TRUNCATED. §9b.2's "Preview of 50 of 1 284"
      pattern: a list that silently stops at 100 is a list somebody will read as complete,
      which for an audit is the one reading that must never happen. %>
  <% if @mail_sends_total > @mail_sends.length %>
    <p class="nodata">
      <%= l(:text_reporter_adhoc_audit_truncated,
            shown: @mail_sends.length, total: @mail_sends_total) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <table class="list">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th><%= l(:label_date) %></th>
        <th><%= l(:field_user) %></th>
        <th><%= l(:label_reporter_template) %></th>
        <th><%= l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail_recipients) %></th>
        <th><%= l(:field_status) %></th>
        <th><%= l(:label_reporter_report_diagnostic_correlation_id) %></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @mail_sends.each do |send| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= format_time(send.created_at) %></td>
          <td><%= link_to_user(User.find_by(id: send.author_id)) %></td>
          <td>
            <%# THE STORED NAME, not the association. A deleted or renamed template would
                otherwise make the audit describe something other than what was sent. %>
            <%= send.template_name.presence || l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail) %>
            <% if send.source.present? %>
              <em class="info">(<%= send.source %>)</em>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td>
            <%#
              THE ADDRESSES ARE THE POINT OF THIS COLUMN. An auditor asking "has anything
              gone to example.com" is asking exactly this, and a count would not answer it.
              A Redmine user is named rather than addressed, because the account already
              says where their mail goes and printing it here would put every colleague's
              address on a page that does not otherwise carry one.
            %>
            <% send.recipients.each do |recipient| %>
              <% if recipient.external? %>
                <div><%= recipient.address %> <em class="info">(<%= l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail_external) %>)</em></div>
              <% else %>
                <%#
                  `.presence`, AND THE `||` ALONE WAS A DEAD GUARD OVER A MISSING KEY —
                  two bugs, one hiding the other, found by an independent review.

                  `link_to_user(nil)` returns `""` (core's helper ends in `h(user.to_s)`),
                  which is TRUTHY, so `||` never fired and a deleted account rendered an
                  empty cell. Underneath it, `label_user_deleted` exists in neither core nor
                  this plugin — it would have printed "Translation missing" the moment the
                  guard worked — while `label_reporter_adhoc_mail_user_gone`, which IS
                  translated into all nine locales, was referenced nowhere in the tree.
                %>
                <div><%= link_to_user(recipient.user).presence ||
                         l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail_user_gone) %></div>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
            <% if send.recipients.empty? %>
              <em class="info"><%= l(:label_none) %></em>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td>
            <%= send.status %>
            <% if send.error.present? %>
              <%# TRUNCATED IN THE CELL AND WHOLE IN THE TITLE. The column is bounded text
                  and an error can be a paragraph; hiding it entirely would remove the one
                  thing the row is consulted for after a failure. %>
              <div><em class="info" title="<%= send.error %>"><%= truncate(send.error, length: 120) %></em></div>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td><%= send.correlation_id %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
<% else %>
  <p class="nodata"><%= l(:label_no_data) %></p>
<% end %>
